#!/usr/bin/python3 -IS
"""tls: tmux sessions and the status of the AI agents in them.

  tls [-v]            print the list once, like ps; -v: vertically
  ttop [-v | -h] [-p] [-f]
                      the same list live and interactive, like top (= tls top);
                      -v vertical, -h horizontal, neither: by the window's
                      shape; -p: n makes its new sessions in the preset layout;
                      -f: ttop fits its tmux pane to the list
  ttv [WINDOW]        add a bar with ttop -v, 25 columns wide and as high as
                      the window, on the right of this tmux window (or
                      WINDOW) if it is 81 columns wide or more; the panes there
                      give up room in proportion to their widths. A window
                      with a ttv bar gets the bar back at 25 columns, and a
                      bar at the bottom moves to the right (= tls bar right)
  tth [WINDOW]        the same with ttop -h, 10 lines high and as wide as the
                      window, at its bottom, if it is 30 lines high or more:
                      for a screen that is taller than wide (= tls bar bottom)
  tls config [--defaults]
                      the settings that tls uses, and the lines of the
                      settings file that it ignores; --defaults: the settings
                      file with each setting at its default, commented out
  tls resume ID|all   bring back a dead agent's tmux session as it was: windows,
                      splits, and each dead agent's resume command in its pane
  tls forget ID       drop a dead agent from the list
  tls new-name        print a free adjective-noun session name (for `t` and n)
  tls preset [WINDOW] give a window with one pane (default: this one) the
                      preset layout: a bar with ttop, two panes side by side
  tls report id=ID [key=value ...]
                      report an agent's state, from any agent; or one JSON object
                      on stdin (the interface: docs/interface.md)
  tls setup           wire up this machine (Claude hooks, tmux hooks, old alias); safe to rerun
  tls hook [claude]   the Claude Code adapter: a hook event on stdin becomes a
                      report (never prints)
  tls seen PANE       tmux hook: you looked at PANE, so its green dot turns grey

Settings: ~/.config/tmux-agents/tls.conf (or TLS_CONFIG) changes the numbers
and the default behaviors below; each one has a default in tls, and an
environment variable wins over the file. `tls config` shows them all.

Columns: session, dot and status, agent name, age, summary. A session with one
agent is one row. A session with no agent or several has a row of its own, and
its agents get a row each under it, from the status column on. An agent's name
is the one its user gave it (in Claude Code: /rename), else the agent's title
for the conversation, else its kind, such as codex; the summary is what it last
said or asked. Dead agents group by the session they ran in. Narrow windows
shrink the names and the summary to fit.

Vertical (-v), for a narrow and tall window: a line with each session's key
and name; under it, for each agent, a line with its dot, status, name and age,
then its summary in a lighter color. The summaries wrap onto more lines while
the whole list fits in the window, and get one line each when it does not.
When the agent lines cannot hold it all, the status words go first (the dot's
color says it too), then the names get cut, then the age goes. ttop with
neither -v nor -h is vertical in a window under 60 columns or taller than wide
(in pixels), else horizontal, and picks again when the window changes size.

Fit (ttop -f), inside tmux: ttop sizes its own pane to the list, with no space
left over, each time the list or the pane changes. Vertical: as wide as its
lines with whole names and status words, 16 to 40 columns and a third of the
window at most. Horizontal: as high as the list and its title, 2 lines to a
third of the window. The preset bars use it.

Resize: when a window changes size, its panes keep their proportions, and a
ttop bar keeps its size; a ttv bar changes too, but stays 25 to 30 columns
wide, and a tth bar 10 to 12 lines high. Back at the old size, the old layout comes back. tmux alone takes the
same number of columns from each pane, so narrow panes go down to 1 column.
To stop it: proportional = off in the [resize] section of the settings file.

Presets (t -p, ttop -p, tls preset): a wide window, such as on a 21:9 monitor
(2.25:1 in pixels or wider, with room for two 80-column panes), gets a bar of
25 columns on the right with ttop -v; any other window (16:9, 16:10) gets a
bar of 5 to 10 lines at the bottom with ttop -h. The rest of the window splits
into two panes side by side. Then the bar's ttop fits the bar to the list
(-f), and the two panes share the change. TLS_PRESET=right or bottom picks a
bar. When tmux does not know the cell size in pixels, tls takes a cell as
twice as high as wide. A rebuilt session gets ttop back in its bar.

ttop keys: ↑/↓ select a row (Home/End, PgUp/PgDn jump) · Enter opens the
selected session or agent pane (switch inside tmux, attach outside; detaching
comes back here), brings back a dead agent's session, or marks a green agent
outside tmux (VS Code) seen · 1-9 open a session at its most urgent agent or
bring back a dead one · n opens a new session (adjective-noun name, current
directory: like `t`; with -p in the preset layout, like `t -p`) · Del or
Backspace deletes the selected session, agent or dead agent (Enter confirms) ·
R brings back all dead agents · d forgets a dead agent: the selected one, or
press its key · q quit. In a window of under 12 lines, such as the bottom bar,
ttop leaves out its blank lines and puts the key hint on the title line.

Dots: red blocked · yellow waiting on you · green done, not looked at yet ·
blue busy (hollow: turn over, background task still running) · grey idle ·
? agent with no status yet · x dead (pane closed or machine rebooted).

State: one JSON file per agent session in ~/.local/state/agent-tls/, mirrored
into the tmux pane options @agent-state and @agent-session. Agents report how
to resume them; [agents] claude in the settings file, or TLS_CLAUDE, sets the
command for Claude (default: claude). How it works: comments at the top of
this file.
"""
# How it works
#
# Any agent tells tls how it's doing with `tls report` (docs/interface.md):
# a session id, a state (busy, waiting, blocked, done, idle, background), a
# summary, a name, and how to resume it. tls finds the agent's process and tmux
# pane from the reporter's environment. Each session's record holds its state,
# cwd, process, boot ID and tmux pane.
#
# `tls hook` is the Claude Code adapter. Claude Code hooks in
# ~/.claude/settings.json (added by `tls setup`) run it on SessionStart,
# UserPromptSubmit, PreToolUse (AskUserQuestion, ExitPlanMode), PostToolUse,
# PermissionRequest, Notification, Stop, StopFailure and SessionEnd; it turns
# each event into a report and reads the session's name and title from its
# transcript. Headless `claude -p` and SDK runs are ignored.
#
# A report with state "finished" hands over the turn's last message, which
# tells blocked, waiting and done apart: a security key or login request means
# blocked, a closing question that isn't just an offer of more work means
# waiting. A tmux-agent-classify file next to this one, if present, decides
# instead. Claude's running background_tasks mean the hollow blue dot.
#
# Green turns grey when you look. When an agent first reports from a tmux pane,
# tls adds five hooks at index [42] to that tmux server (client-attached,
# client-session-changed, session-window-changed, after-select-pane,
# pane-focus-in); they run `tls seen` only if the pane's agent is green. Opening
# a session from ttop, Enter on an agent outside tmux, or typing a prompt also
# clears green. Switching VS Code tabs doesn't count: tmux reports every
# attached client as focused.
#
# Resize in proportion. When a window changes size, tmux itself takes the same
# number of columns from each pane, until a pane is down to 1 column: a narrow
# pane, such as a ttop bar, goes first. So tls adds two more hooks at [42].
# window-layout-changed keeps the layout that you made in the window option
# @tls-base (tmux alone does this, with no process): not one that tmux made
# for a new size, which is not the size in @tls-size, and not the one that tls
# applied (@tls-applied). window-resized runs `tls relayout`, which scales
# @tls-base to the new size: each split keeps its proportions, and a ttop bar
# keeps its size along its split; a ttv bar (without -f) changes in proportion
# too, within [ttv] min-width and max-width. Back at the old size, the old
# layout comes back exactly. [resize] proportional = off stops it. The hooks
# come with a mark, @tls-hooks: when an agent's state changes and the mark of
# its tmux server is not the one of this tls (a new server, an update, a new
# setting), tls adds its hooks again.
#
# An agent is dead when its process is gone (PID plus start time) or the boot ID
# changed. A deliberate quit (/exit, /clear, logout) deletes the record; a kill
# (tmux gone, reboot, closed terminal) keeps it, and resume recreates the tmux
# session in the old cwd and types `claude --resume <id>`. Dead records go after
# 14 days, or once Claude's transcript is gone.
#
# Hooks and ttop also save the structure of each tmux session an agent runs or
# ran in (windows, layout strings, each pane's directory and agent) to
# layouts.json, keyed by tmux server, so a reboot can't overwrite it. While a
# dead agent has lost its pane, its session keeps the structure from before.
# Bringing an agent back: if its pane sits at a shell prompt, resume types
# there. Else its session is rebuilt if it's gone (every window and pane; other
# panes come back as shells in their old directories), or its pane is split
# back into its window, and the saved layout puts the splits back. With nothing
# saved, it gets a new window.
#
# Del kills a session or an agent's pane once you confirm. The agents' records
# are marked deleted first, so the dying Claude's last hooks can't list them as
# dead; a later `claude --resume` of one clears the mark.
#
# Files: tls, ttop (runs `tls top`), ttv and tth (`tls bar`) and tls-test
# (self-test on throwaway tmux servers) in one directory, plus tmux-agent-classify if you have one. Install
# them as regular files, not symlinks (install.sh does): dotfile syncers often
# skip symlinks. Hook errors go to errors.log in the state directory; hooks
# never print.
#
# Uninstall: delete the `tls hook` entries from ~/.claude/settings.json and the
# files, and restore the alias from its comment in ~/.bashrc if setup left one.
# tmux drops the [42] hooks when its server restarts.
import json
import os
import re
import shlex
import shutil
import socket
import subprocess
import sys
import time
import unicodedata

sys.dont_write_bytecode = True  # loading the classifier must not leave __pycache__ in ~/bin

HOME = os.path.expanduser("~")
STATE_DIR = os.environ.get("TLS_STATE_DIR") or os.path.join(HOME, ".local", "state", "agent-tls")
TMUX_ARGS = os.environ.get("TLS_TMUX", "").split()  # e.g. "-L test", for tests
SELF = os.path.realpath(__file__)
CLASSIFIER = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(SELF), "tmux-agent-classify")
HOST = socket.gethostname()
SID_RE = re.compile(r"[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9_.:-]{0,119}")  # a session id; also a file name
DELIBERATE_END = {"prompt_input_exit", "clear", "logout"}  # SessionEnd reasons: user quit on purpose
RESUME_GRACE = 120
DELETED_TTL = 60  # a record deleted in ttop stays this long, to swallow its dying agent's hooks
SHELLS = {"bash", "zsh", "sh", "dash", "fish", "ksh", "tcsh"}  # a pane at a prompt: safe to type into
SERVER_ID = "#{pid}:#{start_time}"  # one tmux server's life: pane and window ids restart with it

# ── settings ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Each setting has its default here. The settings file (config_path) changes
# any of them, and an environment variable in SETTING_ENV wins over both.
# `tls config --defaults` prints the file that install.sh puts in place, with
# each setting at its default and commented out (config/tls.conf).

TTOP_FLAGS = ("-v", "-h", "-p", "-f", "--vertical", "--horizontal", "--preset", "--fit")
SETTINGS = (  # (section, key, default, kind, help); kind: words to pick from, int, float, str, "words" or "ttop"
    ("ttop", "layout", "auto", ("auto", "vertical", "horizontal"),
     "The layout of ttop with neither -v nor -h. auto: vertical in a narrow window, "
     "or in a window taller than wide, else horizontal."),
    ("ttop", "narrow", 60, int, "auto: ttop is vertical in a window narrower than this many columns."),
    ("ttop", "short", 12, int,
     "In a window with fewer lines than this, ttop has no blank lines, and its key hint is on the title line."),
    ("ttop", "fit-min-width", 16, int, "ttop -f: the fewest columns of a vertical pane."),
    ("ttop", "fit-max-width", 40, int, "ttop -f: the most columns of a vertical pane. It also gets a third of the window at most."),
    ("ttv", "width", 25, int, "The width of the bar that ttv adds, in columns."),
    ("ttv", "min-width", 25, int,
     "When the window changes size, the bar changes in proportion, but it keeps this many columns at least."),
    ("ttv", "max-width", 30, int, "When the window changes size, the bar gets this many columns at most."),
    ("ttv", "min-window", 81, int, "ttv adds a bar only to a window at least this many columns wide."),
    ("ttv", "options", "-v", "ttop",
     "The ttop options of the bar. With -f, ttop fits the bar to the list, and min-width and max-width do not apply."),
    ("tth", "height", 10, int, "The height of the bar that tth adds, in lines."),
    ("tth", "min-height", 10, int,
     "When the window changes size, the bar changes in proportion, but it keeps this many lines at least."),
    ("tth", "max-height", 12, int, "When the window changes size, the bar gets this many lines at most."),
    ("tth", "min-window", 30, int, "tth adds a bar only to a window at least this many lines high."),
    ("tth", "options", "-h", "ttop",
     "The ttop options of the bar. With -f, ttop fits the bar to the list, and min-height and max-height do not apply."),
    ("preset", "layout", "auto", ("auto", "right", "bottom"),
     "Where t -p, ttop -p and tls preset put the bar. auto: on the right of a wide window, else at the bottom."),
    ("preset", "wide", 2.25, float,
     "A window is wide from this width/height ratio in pixels on: 21:9 is 2.33, 16:9 is 1.78."),
    ("preset", "pane-width", 80, int, "A wide window must also have room for two panes this many columns wide next to the bar."),
    ("preset", "right-width", 25, int, "The width of a bar on the right, until its ttop fits it to the list."),
    ("preset", "bottom-min-lines", 5, int,
     "A bar at the bottom starts at a fifth of the window, with this many lines at least, until its ttop fits it."),
    ("preset", "bottom-max-lines", 10, int, "A bar at the bottom starts with this many lines at most."),
    ("resize", "proportional", "on", ("on", "off"),
     "on: when a window changes size, its panes keep their proportions, and a ttop bar keeps its size "
     "(ttv and tth bars: see [ttv] and [tth]). off: tmux takes the same number of columns from each pane."),
    ("agents", "claude", "claude", str, "The command that resumes a Claude Code session, as: claude --resume ID."),
    ("agents", "commands", "claude codex gemini", "words",
     "A pane that runs one of these commands shows as an agent before the agent reports."),
    ("agents", "keep-dead-days", 14, int, "A dead agent stays in the list this many days, or until its transcript goes."),
)
SETTING_ENV = {"TLS_PRESET": ("preset", "layout"), "TLS_CLAUDE": ("agents", "claude"),
               "TLS_AGENT_COMMANDS": ("agents", "commands")}
SETTING_PAIRS = ((("ttop", "fit-min-width"), ("ttop", "fit-max-width")), (("ttv", "min-width"), ("ttv", "max-width")),
                 (("tth", "min-height"), ("tth", "max-height")),
                 (("preset", "bottom-min-lines"), ("preset", "bottom-max-lines")))
_CONF = None  # (values, sources, problems), when a setting is first needed


def config_path():
    return os.environ.get("TLS_CONFIG") or os.path.join(
        os.environ.get("XDG_CONFIG_HOME") or os.path.join(HOME, ".config"), "tmux-agents", "tls.conf")


def setting(text, kind):
    """text as a value of this kind of setting; ValueError if it is not one."""
    text = text.strip()
    if isinstance(kind, tuple):
        if text.lower() not in kind:
            raise ValueError("use " + " or ".join(kind))
        return text.lower()
    if kind in (int, float):
        try:
            n = kind(text)
        except ValueError:
            raise ValueError("use a %snumber" % ("whole " if kind is int else "")) from None
        if not n > 0:
            raise ValueError("use a number over 0")
        return n
    words = text.split()
    if not words:
        raise ValueError("use a value")
    bad = [w for w in words if kind == "ttop" and w not in TTOP_FLAGS]
    if bad:
        raise ValueError("ttop has no option %s" % bad[0])
    return tuple(words) if kind in ("words", "ttop") else text


def load_conf():
    """(values, sources, problems): each setting from its default, then the
    settings file, then the environment; sources: where a value that is not
    the default comes from; problems: what tls ignored, and why."""
    import configparser
    kinds = {(s, k): kind for s, k, _, kind, _ in SETTINGS}
    values = {(s, k): setting(str(d), kind) for s, k, d, kind, _ in SETTINGS}
    sources, problems, found, path = {}, [], [], config_path()
    parser = configparser.ConfigParser(inline_comment_prefixes=("#",), interpolation=None, strict=False)
    try:
        with open(path) as f:
            parser.read_file(f)
        found = [(sec, key, text, "%s [%s]" % (os.path.basename(path), sec))
                 for sec in parser.sections() for key, text in parser.items(sec)]
    except FileNotFoundError:
        pass
    except (OSError, UnicodeError, configparser.Error) as e:
        problems.append("%s: %s" % (path, " ".join(str(e).split())))
    found += [(sk[0], sk[1], os.environ[var], var) for var, sk in SETTING_ENV.items() if os.environ.get(var)]
    for sec, key, text, where in found:
        if (sec, key) not in kinds:
            problems.append("%s: no setting %s" % (where, key))
            continue
        try:
            values[sec, key], sources[sec, key] = setting(text, kinds[sec, key]), where
        except ValueError as e:
            problems.append("%s: %s = %s: %s" % (where, key, text.strip(), e))
    for lo, hi in SETTING_PAIRS:
        if values[lo] > values[hi]:
            problems.append("%s %s is more than %s %s: both stay at their defaults" % (lo[1], values[lo], hi[1], values[hi]))
            for sk in (lo, hi):
                values[sk] = setting(str(next(d for s, k, d, _, _ in SETTINGS if (s, k) == sk)), kinds[sk])
                sources.pop(sk, None)
    return values, sources, problems


def conf(section, key):
    """The value of a setting (load_conf, once for each process)."""
    global _CONF
    if _CONF is None:
        try:
            _CONF = load_conf()
        except Exception as e:  # a hook must never fail over its settings
            _CONF = ({(s, k): setting(str(d), kind) for s, k, d, kind, _ in SETTINGS}, {},
                     ["%s: %s" % (config_path(), e)])
    return _CONF[0][section, key]


def conf_problems():
    conf("ttop", "layout")
    return _CONF[2]


def config_text(defaults=False):
    """tls config: the settings file with the value of each setting that tls
    uses; defaults: with each setting at its default and commented out."""
    import textwrap
    note = lambda text: textwrap.wrap(text, 78, initial_indent="# ", subsequent_indent="# ")
    show = lambda v: " ".join(v) if isinstance(v, tuple) else str(v)
    if defaults:
        lines = note("Settings of tmux-agents: tls, ttop, ttv and the presets. tls reads them from "
                     "~/.config/tmux-agents/tls.conf, or from the file that TLS_CONFIG names.") + ["#"] + \
            note("Each setting shows its default value. To change one, remove the # in front of it and "
                 "change the value. ttop and tmux hooks read the file when they start. These environment "
                 "variables win over the file: %s. `tls config` shows the values that tls uses, and the lines "
                 "that it ignores." % ", ".join("%s ([%s] %s)" % (v, sk[0], sk[1]) for v, sk in SETTING_ENV.items()))
    else:
        conf("ttop", "layout")
        lines = ["# The settings that tls uses: the defaults, then %s, then the environment." % config_path()]
        lines += ["# ignored: " + problem for problem in _CONF[2]]
    section = None
    for sec, key, default, kind, text in SETTINGS:
        if sec != section:
            lines += ["", "[%s]" % sec]
            section = sec
        if defaults:
            lines += note(text) + ["# %s = %s" % (key, show(setting(str(default), kind)))]
        else:
            where = _CONF[1].get((sec, key))
            lines.append("%s = %s%s" % (key, show(conf(sec, key)), "  # from " + where if where else ""))
    return "\n".join(lines) + "\n"


